# Rover 200 R8 (five-door hatchback), 1989-1995.
#
# The R8 was the joint Austin Rover / Honda design that replaced the Maestro as
# well as the SD3, launched on 11 October 1989 as the first car of the newly
# privatised Rover Group. It was the first Rover with the K-series engine and won
# What Car? Car of the Year. Hatchback and saloon were built to 1995; the coupe,
# cabriolet and Tourer continued to 1999.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rover_200_/_25
# https://rover200.org.uk/cars/r8/r8-history
#
# Dimensions 4.220 x 1.680 x 1.400 m, wheelbase 2.550 m; the published R8 dimension
# box, which the 3-door and 5-door share. The 1992 facelift changed the grille and
# lamp detail only, so the pre-facelift car is modelled for the whole run.
#
# Colours: Flame Red (BLVC 818), Silver Leaf metallic (421), White Diamond (655)
# and Moonraker Blue metallic (424), all Rover UK offerings of 1989-92. DVSA
# first-registration shares begin at 1994, so the split is era-adjusted from the
# 1994-96 measured base, which this car's own years sit close to: red 7, silver 6,
# white 5, blue 4. Total 22. Provisional pending the central rebase.
#
# Speed 175 km/h, the 214 Si's tier; the 220 Turbo was far quicker but is a trim
# variant, not a separate object. Comfort 90/465, on the line 330 + 1.5 x 90: the
# R8's refinement and build quality were the reasons the press gave it Car of the
# Year. Classes 0,1,2, since it was priced above the mainstream hatchbacks.
#
# Dates are road presence, not production: October 1989 intro, retires staggered
# 2001-2005 on a twelve- to sixteen-year tail.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/rover-200-r8.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/rover-200-r8.dae (56 objects). References:
# citycars/references/rover-200-r8/ (gitignored; Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0).
#
# Accepted low-poly deviations, recorded rather than corrected: the faceted
# surfaces stand in for the photographed compound curves, the lamp detail is
# simplified, and the two-tone grey lower body of the 216 GSi reference is carried
# on all four colourways because it is a single shared shell rather than a trim
# variant.

